VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: HC1
ATTEMPT: 2
---
Setup: cp -a of the full tree to a scratch dir (kept .git); built from source;
server run only on 127.0.0.1:18096 from freshly-built binaries in /tmp; never
touched .swarm/tier3/HC1/* or ran accept.sh; never ran a prebuilt
./budget2/./server; port 8080 never touched.

ATTACK 1 — re-run attempt-1 attack + boundary variants (zero HI rows,
coverage-after-window, window-ends-day-before-earliest-bill, coverage-start
exactly on window start/end). All PASS — MCP and dashboard agree in every
case:
  - zeroHI (2026-01-01..2026-06-30): dashboard "No premium target set", no
    "Health:" line, no NaN; MCP healthcare_monthly_target/actual/delta all 0,
    combined_cumulative_delta 1253.39 (living-only, matches).
  - afterWindow (bill 2026-08-05, window ends 06-30): same suppression on
    both surfaces, byte-identical to zeroHI case.
  - dayBefore (window ends 2026-05-04, bill on 2026-05-05): suppressed on
    both surfaces.
  - onWindowStart (coverage start == rangeStart): $961.18/mo, target $1,000,
    consistent on both surfaces — correct non-degenerate clip.
  - onWindowEnd (coverage start == rangeEnd, single 1-day covered span):
    BOTH surfaces render an extreme $27,393.75/mo actual (900 / 0.0329
    months). Verified this is `MonthsBetween`'s pre-existing "+1 day, floor
    at 1 day" inclusive-range convention (internal/services/metrics/
    metrics.go:25-31), reused unmodified by HC1's `ClippedHealthcareMonths`
    per spec instruction ("through the same MonthsBetween helper"); the same
    floor already produces equally extreme rates for MonthsInRange on any
    1-day query window today. Both surfaces agree and neither is NaN/Inf, so
    no cross-surface (split-classification) violation — flagging as a
    BACKLOG OBSERVATION (pre-existing MonthsBetween degenerate-range
    behavior, not introduced or worsened by HC1), not a FAIL.

ATTACK 2 — does zeroing corrupt anything else (months_in_range, living
fields, combined_cumulative_delta)? No corruption found in any state above:
MonthsInRange always finite and unaffected (it stays on the unclipped
windows-in-range basis, per spec); living fields track only budgetTarget
independently; CombinedCumulativeDelta = cumulativeDelta + healthcareCumulativeDelta,
and healthcareCumulativeDelta is provably 0 whenever coverageMonths is 0
(`healthcareTotal - healthcareTarget*0`), confirmed both by reading
metrics.go:307-310 and by the live zeroHI/afterWindow/dayBefore probes above
(combined delta = living-only figure exactly). No state found where
HasHealthcareTarget is true with stale fields.

ATTACK 3 — enumerated every non-test reader of HealthcareTarget/Actual/
PerMonthDelta/CumulativeDelta/TargetTotal via
`grep -rn "HealthcareTarget\b|HealthcareActual\b|HealthcarePerMonthDelta\b|
HealthcareCumulativeDelta\b|HealthcareTargetTotal\b" --include=*.go
--include=*.html | grep -v _test.go`. Readers: kpis.html (gated on
HasHealthcareTarget for the delta/target lines; HealthcareActual itself is
shown unconditionally but as "$0.00" when unconfigured — cosmetic, matches
today's convention), verdict.go's classifyBucket (gated on HasHealthcareTarget,
confirmed by code read), metrics.go itself (the single source). No new
unconditional reader found; the attempt-1 gating in Calculate + the
attempt-2 gating in summary.go together cover every surface. PASS.

ATTACK 4 (the decisive one) — mutation honesty at the DASHBOARD's own call
sites, not just the summarize_spending path the attempt-2 manifest targets.

Ruling 2026-08-30b's own quoted mutation text — "replacing
`HealthcareCoverageStart(data.Active())` with `(filtered)` ... or `(data)`"
— is the LITERAL source at internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers.go lines 99,
184, 245 (handleDashboard, handleKPIsPartial, handleChartData's
budget-vs-actual case), not internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/summary.go (whose
call site reads `metrics.HealthcareCoverageStart(ts)` after a `ts =
ts.Active()` reassignment, a syntactically different pattern, unchanged
since attempt 1).

Attempt 2's manifest lists exactly two new mutation-killing tests, both in
internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/summary_test.go (the MCP/summarize_spending
path only). I re-ran the ruling's exact mutation at the actual quoted
location instead:

  sed -i 's/metrics.HealthcareCoverageStart(data.Active())/metrics.HealthcareCoverageStart(filtered)/g' internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers.go
  go build ./...        # clean
  go test ./...          # ALL packages print "ok" -- 0 failures anywhere

  (restored, then) sed -i 's/.../metrics.HealthcareCoverageStart(data)/g' ...  # duplicates-included variant
  go build ./... && go test ./...   # ALL packages print "ok" again -- 0 failures

Both of the ruling's named mutations — full-ledger-vs-window AND
duplicates-included — survive the ENTIRE go test suite when applied at the
three handlers.go call sites that actually feed the live dashboard's Budget
card, Healthcare card, verdict bar, and budget-vs-actual chart (criterion 4's
named consumers). Confirmed by reading
internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers_http_test.go (the file with real
HTTP-level tests for handleDashboard/handleKPIsPartial/handleChartData): its
only fixture (`defaultRows()`) contains zero Health Insurance category rows,
and grepping the whole file for "Health Insurance"/"HealthInsurance" matches
nothing outside the budget-vs-actual chart-container-presence tests — so no
existing test, at any tier, can tell a full-ledger-derived coverage start
apart from a window-filtered or duplicates-included one at these three call
sites.

I also confirmed the oracle itself is blind to this, per the ruling's own
diagnosis, by manually reproducing its Stage-2 HTTP checks (build a fresh
binary with the mutation, run on 127.0.0.1:18096 against a COPY of
.swarm/tier3/HC1/fixture/data — original untouched, accept.sh never
executed) for the "window-filtered" mutation:
  dashboard/kpis: "Health: $1,800.00 of $1,872.69", "since May 5, 2026",
  "over plan for this period" -- MCP: combined_cumulative_delta 1180.7,
  healthcare_monthly_actual 961.18 -- IDENTICAL to attempt-2's own recorded
  oracle.2.log values. The fixture's only Health Insurance bill (2026-05-05)
  already lies inside the query window (2026-01-01..2026-06-30), so
  window-filtering it changes nothing — exactly the blindness the ruling
  already named, now reproduced at the location the ruling's own quoted code
  came from.

Weighing (per the task's explicit instruction to justify either way):
Criterion 3 reads "Unit tests cover: ... full-ledger (not window)
derivation, duplicates excluded" with no call site named, and ruling
2026-08-30b's directive was "Attempt 2 must add mutation-killing tests for
both" -- not "for the summarize_spending path." The two properties are
call-site wiring-correctness properties (the exported `HealthcareCoverageStart`
function itself has no notion of "window" or "call site" -- it just processes
whatever TransactionSet it is handed), so a test of the function in isolation
cannot stand in for testing that each of the enumerated consumers wires it
correctly. HC1's own consumer enumeration (HC-RUN-SPEC.md lines 49-71) names
handlers.go explicitly and separately from summary.go as its own numbered
consumer (#3) alongside metrics.go (#1) and summary.go (#4) -- three
independent wiring sites, only one of which now has regression protection.
The three unprotected call sites are not a peripheral surface: they are the
literal rendering path for the Budget KPI card, Healthcare KPI card, verdict
bar, and budget-vs-actual chart -- the primary consumers criterion 4 names.
Given the split-classification precedent (ruling 2026-08-29a: "the checker's
job is to ENUMERATE the surfaces ... not trust the diff") and that this is
the exact defect class ruling 2026-08-30b already required fixed this
attempt, I weigh this as a genuine, unresolved criterion-3 gap and not a
mere residual-risk note -- this is a FAIL, not a backlog item.

ATTACK 5 (other smells) — refund-only ledger (covered by attack 1's zeroHI
family and the existing refund-must-not-count tests); fractional-cent
rendered-sum invariant untouched by attempt 2 (attempt 1's
verdict_fractional_cent_test.go still present, not touched, `go test
./internal/handlers/dashboard/...` green). No new smell found beyond ATTACK 4.

BACKLOG OBSERVATIONS (not grounds for FAIL, in-scope-but-pre-existing or
theoretical):
1. MonthsBetween's inclusive "+1 day, floor 1" convention produces an
   extreme implied monthly rate when the covered span collapses to exactly
   one day (coverage start == window end). Pre-existing function, reused per
   spec instruction, both surfaces agree, no NaN/Inf — flag for a future
   task, not HC1.
2. HasCombinedTarget = combinedTarget>0 (budgetTarget+healthcareTarget,
   UNCLIPPED) gates the verdict card's visibility independently of whether
   either bucket is actually accruing this window. In the theoretical case
   budgetTarget==0 AND healthcareTarget configured with zero coverage in the
   window, HasCombinedTarget could read true (healthcareTarget alone > 0)
   while TargetTotal (clipped) reads 0, producing a "$X over, red" verdict
   card with TargetTotal<=0. This requires living target itself to be 0,
   which does not occur on the live plan and is not one of HC1's edge cases;
   this gate is unchanged by HC1 (pre-existing `combinedTarget>0` logic).
   Not reproduced live; theoretical only; flagged for the backlog, not a
   FAIL.

STRONGEST ATTACK: Attack 4 (dashboard-handler mutation survival). Both of
ruling 2026-08-30b's named mutations, applied verbatim at the literal call
site the ruling quoted, survive `go build ./...` clean and `go test ./...`
green across every package, and (by direct reproduction of the fixture
arithmetic) would also survive the oracle. This is the same defect class the
prior attempt was already faulted for, now fixed at one call site
(summarize_spending) while three siblings supplying the primary dashboard
surfaces remain unguarded.
